The '''Mission Saint James of the Kuarupíba''' ({{langx|bl|Misión Santiago de Los Kuarupíba}}) is a historic [[W:Basilicas in the Catholic Church|Catholic basilica]] complex in the [[Santiago]] barrio of [[Ampurias]], within the [[Castejón|Castejón Metropolis]]. It is dedicated to [[W:James the Great|James the Great]], the historic [[W:Patron saint|patron]] of Bayara. Operated by the [[Diocese of San José in Alpujarras]], the mission was founded in the 17th century by missionaries of the [[W:Jesuit Order|Jesuit Order]].

== History ==
Within the [[Alpujarra]], the Jesuits established eight reductions, collectively often called the [[Alpujarran missions]]. By 1641, despite Spanish incursions and epidemics, the Kuarupíba population of the Alpujarran missions was 36,190. For nearly two centuries thereafter, the mission population increased to a maximum of 227,562 in 1832. Populations of individual reductions varied from 2,000 to 7,000.

The immediate need of both the settlers the indigenous peoples in the 1640s was to protect themselves from Spanish explorers and authorities. The Jesuits began to arm them, producing guns and gunpowder in the missions. They also secured the support of the [[Hesperian League|Hesperians]], and some arms, to raise militias of Indigenous people to defend the Alpujarra against raids. The militias would eventually number as many as 4,000 troops and their cavalry was especially effective, wearing European-style uniforms and carrying bows and arrows as well as muskets.

